From: Sobs <antohasobne@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] wined3d: DISCARD/NOOVERWRITE buffer maps through heap memory
 ("HeapMaps")

WineD3D accelerates DISCARD and NOOVERWRITE maps of dynamic buffers with
persistently mapped buffer objects allocated from the application thread.
Under WoW64 Wine hides GL_ARB_buffer_storage from 32-bit programs unless
it has a Vulkan device or GL_AMD_pinned_memory, so there every such map
went through the CS thread and waited for it to drain: in a sea battle
of Sid Meier's Pirates! about 100 maps a frame, the game's thread
spinning 15-17% of its time and the render thread idle in between.

With the new setting HeapMaps (WINE_D3D_CONFIG=HeapMaps=1 or the
Direct3D registry key) such maps return heap memory that the CS thread
uploads on unmap, as UpdateSubresource() does; a DISCARD map uploads
the whole buffer (so a busy buffer object is renamed, not waited for).
The program has to write the whole range it maps. HeapMaps=2 also takes
write-only maps without flags of buffers not pinned in system memory;
Pirates! crashes with that (it keeps or reads the pointer), so it is
only for programs known to work with it. Default 0: unchanged.

Pirates! sea battle: the render thread busy 84% -> 97%, 22-28 -> 25-31
fps.

Maps return the buffer's own copy in heap memory, kept until the buffer
is destroyed, and unmaps upload a copy of the mapped range: d3d9
programs may draw from a buffer they still have mapped
(wined3d_device_context_flush_mapped_buffer(), which then also uploads
a copy).

d3d9 passes a Lock() of size 0, "to the end of the buffer", as an empty
box, which wined3d lets through for buffers. Daemonica locks its ring
of text vertices like that, one line of text at a time; uploading the
empty box sent nothing, so text was drawn from the vertices of an
earlier lap of the ring (missing shadows, flickering lines), and the
first version of this, mapping memory of the box's size, let the game
write past it (random crashes, taken then for writes after the unmap).
Such maps now cover the rest of the buffer. As the program writes only
a little of it, a copy of what was uploaded (the mirror, made by the
first such map) tells what changed; large heap copies are allocated with
write watches, so only the pages written since are compared.

The uploads of NOOVERWRITE maps, which by contract overwrite nothing
the GPU may still read, write through an unsynchronised map of just
their range (GL_ARB_map_buffer_range) instead of glBufferSubData():
Mesa's panfrost answers a write into a buffer object read by a pending
batch with a flush and a copy of the whole buffer object into a new
one. Daemonica's first location: 36 -> 47 fps, its text drawn right.

